{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":231009,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"329692a0-2ef4-4644-9805-d11165d8ed18","total_cost_usd":1.2502085000000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":69466,"cache_read_input_tokens":20655,"output_tokens":19748,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":69466,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":19748,"cache_read_input_tokens":20655,"cache_creation_input_tokens":69466,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":69466},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":51436,"outputTokens":15,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.051511,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":19748,"cacheReadInputTokens":20655,"cacheCreationInputTokens":69466,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.1986975000000002,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"The catalog is internally clean and sixteen of eighteen checks hold, but the skim heat multiplier the entire ablation economy is priced off is 0.13-0.24 away from what was flown in the high band, and two quantities were made to pass by bending the measurement rather than the design.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_names_consistent\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band name used in the catalog or the params appears in the baseline's bands, and no band is referenced that the baseline does not define.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"baseline.bands = {suborbital, low, high}. Catalog debris[].band uses exactly {suborbital, low, high} across all 24 pieces and band_summary[].band the same three. Params reference the same three in ablation.heat_index, ablation.cost_curve, ablation.optimal_skims and economy.band_value_multiplier. Set difference in both directions is empty.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_recomputed\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises: piece_count, mean_mass_kg and fragile_fraction recomputed.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 8 pieces; masses 140+380+460+60+110+95+70+1150 = 2465, /8 = 308.125 (claimed 308.125); spawn weights 20+14+9+18+16+5+4+1 = 87, fragile weights 5+4 = 9, 9/87 = 0.1034 (claimed 0.103). Low: 8 pieces; 500+380+300+450+260+240+210+2000 = 4340, /8 = 542.5 (claimed 542.5); weights 67, fragile 5+4+3 = 12, 12/67 = 0.1791 (claimed 0.179). High: 8 pieces; 1000+820+1150+540+900+430+980+2900 = 8720, /8 = 1090.0 (claimed 1090); weights 56, fragile 6+4+3+1 = 14, 14/56 = 0.2500 (claimed 0.25). All nine figures match.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mean_mass_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"The catalog's mean piece mass rises band over band, so valuable junk physically fights you.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"308.125 -> 542.5 -> 1090.0 kg, i.e. x1.76 then x2.01, x3.54 end to end. Excluding the three crane-only oversized pieces it still rises: (2465-1150)/7 = 187.9, (4340-2000)/7 = 334.3, (8720-2900)/7 = 831.4 kg, x1.78 then x2.49. Monotone under both readings.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises across the bands, roughly 1 in 10 suborbital to 1 in 4 high.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted fragile share 9/87 = 0.1034, 12/67 = 0.1791, 14/56 = 0.2500. That is 1-in-9.7 rising to exactly 1-in-4, monotone, and lands on the GDD's stated endpoints to within 3%.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted over every hand-tetherable piece in the shipping slice (suborbital + low), charging fragile pieces their uncrushed slot cost because fragile is never crushable: mass-weight sum = 39475 kg, slot-weight sum = 231 slots, so 170.9 kg per slot. 6 slots x 170.9 = 1025.4 kg against dry_mass_kg 1200 gives 2225.4 kg loaded = 1.854x. The simulator measures the same quantity at 170.89 kg/slot, 1025.3 kg, ratio 1.85. Deficit from doubling is 7.3%, inside the 25% band.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"high_band_optimum_is_one_or_two_skims\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The cheapest descent is 1 to 2 skims from the high band; no band's optimum exceeds it; each minimum equals optimal_skims; and the curve is what the stated model produces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Minima by index: suborbital [14.03, 14.60, 18.71, 27.40] -> 0; low [24.68, 21.62, 23.56, 31.63] -> 1; high [57.37, 43.12, 38.41, 44.58] -> 2. High is 2, inside 1-2; the sequence 0/1/2 is non-decreasing with altitude so no band exceeds high; optimal_skims {0, 1, 2} matches index-for-index. Recomputing the model cost(k) = 0.00035*(heat_index*mult[k])^2.4 + 2.6*(1.5^(k+1)-1)/0.5 + k*0.00035*22^2.4, with the skim term 0.00035*22^2.4 = 0.583: suborbital k=0 gives 11.43+2.60 = 14.03 (array 14.03) and k=3 gives 4.53+21.13+1.75 = 27.41 (array 27.40); low k=0 gives 22.08+2.60 = 24.68 (array 24.68) and k=1 gives 14.53+6.50+0.58 = 21.61 (array 21.62); high k=0 gives 54.76+2.60 = 57.36 (array 57.37) and k=2 gives 24.90+12.35+1.17 = 38.42 (array 38.41). Six recomputed points, all within 0.1%. Noted for the record and handled by skim_multiplier_matches_flown: the flown descents put cheapest_pass_count at 1 in all nine cases, so these interior minima exist only in the model's assumed multipliers.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_multiplier_matches_flown\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed skim_heat_multiplier matches what was flown; more than 0.10 apart at any index in the high band fails.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Claimed ablation.skim_heat_multiplier [1, 0.84, 0.72, 0.68] against skims.high.skim_heat_multiplier_measured [1, 0.971, 0.944, 0.918] at the same entry depth (0 m). Index-by-index gaps: 0.000, 0.131, 0.224, 0.238 — three of four exceed the 0.10 tolerance, the worst by 2.4x. The params claim one high-band skim removes 16% of committed-entry peak heat; the simulator removes 2.9%. Low band is no better ([1, 0.956, 0.912, 0.867], gaps 0.116/0.192/0.187) and only suborbital is close ([1, 0.898, 0.730, 0.730], gaps 0.058/0.010/0.050). Because every entry in cost_curve is computed from this array, the entire skim economy is mispriced: recomputing high-band k=2 with the measured 0.944 gives 0.00035*(146*0.944)^2.4 + 12.35 + 1.17 = 49.6+13.5 = 63.1 against the array's 38.41, which moves the high band's minimum to index 0 and destroys the 1-2 skim optimum the previous check passed on paper.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Adopt the measured skim_heat_multiplier per band from skims.<band>.skim_heat_multiplier_measured and re-derive cost_curve and optimal_skims from it; if the optimum then collapses to 0 skims everywhere, the lever to move is ballistic coefficient or entry depth, not the multiplier array.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_curve_saturates_and_toll_grows\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"skim_heat_multiplier starts at 1.0, is non-increasing and flattens by its last two entries; cycle_toll_growth exceeds 1.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"[1, 0.84, 0.72, 0.68]: first entry exactly 1.0; differences -0.16, -0.12, -0.04 are all non-positive so the array is non-increasing; the final step of 0.04 is one third of the preceding 0.12 and one quarter of the first, so the benefit saturates rather than continuing to fall. cycle_toll_growth = 1.5 > 1, giving tolls 2.60, 6.50, 12.35, 21.13 for 1-4 cycles — a superlinear fatigue term that bounds indefinite skimming (the 4th cycle alone costs 8.78 points against a maximum remaining skim saving of about 3 points in any band).\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_full_hold_soft_landing\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Parachute descent speed at full hold is under the 5 m/s soft-landing threshold but above about 3.5 m/s, so the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"rho0*Cd*A = 1.0 * 1.85 * 550 = 1017.5 m^2. At the measured full hold m = 1200 + 1025.3 = 2225.3 kg and g = 4.0: v = sqrt(2*2225.3*4/1017.5) = sqrt(17.496) = 4.18 m/s, and the simulator's flown touchdown is 4.18 m/s in all three bands. That is 0.82 m/s (16%) under soft_landing_ms 5 and 0.68 m/s above the 3.5 m/s floor. Empty is sqrt(2*1200*4/1017.5) = 3.07 m/s, matching the flown 3.07, so descent speed grows as sqrt(mass) as 2.3.2 requires. The params' stated descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.3 is 0.12 high because it assumed 1149.9 kg of cargo rather than the catalog's 1025.3, but both sides of the threshold test hold either way.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps_at_half\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee clamps at exactly 50%, is zero inside the free radius, is linear between, and can never go negative or exceed the cap.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"max_fee_fraction = 0.5 exactly, curve = 'linear', free_radius_m = 1200. Fee = 0.5 * clamp((d - 1200)/(785398 - 1200), 0, 1) * haul value, where half the circumference is pi*250000 = 785398.16 m from baseline.planet.radius_m. At d = 0 and d = 1200 the clamped argument is 0 so fee = 0; at d = 785398 it is exactly 1 so fee = 0.500 of haul value; at the midpoint d = 393299 it is 0.5 so fee = 0.250 — linear. The clamp at both ends makes negative fees and fees above 0.5 unreachable for any d, so payout stays in [50%, 100%] and never reaches savings.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Launch cost is at or below the value of three cheap suborbital pieces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Cheapest thing in the sky is a solid suborbital small: size_class_base_value.small 50 x band_value_multiplier.suborbital 1.0 = 50. Three of them = 150. launch_cost = 125 <= 150, i.e. 2.5 cheap pieces, inside the GDD's 'about 2-3'. A six-slot lazy haul of commonest junk grosses 300 against 125 launch plus replating at replate_cost_per_pct 3 x the flown suborbital direct-entry ablation of 12.1% = 36.30, total cost 161.30, net +138.70.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"steady_tow_never_shears\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Steady-state towing tension never reaches magnet_hold_force_n, at any legal load.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"a = jetpack_thrust_n/(suit_mass_kg + towed) = 520/(130 + towed); per-cable tension = m_piece * a = 520*m_piece/(130 + towed), which is strictly below 520 N for any load because m_piece <= towed. Heaviest legal two-piece hand-tetherable load is two iridium_battery_stack at 1150 kg = 2300 kg: a = 520/2430 = 0.214 m/s^2, tension = 1150 * 0.214 = 246.1 N against magnet_hold_force_n 900 — a 3.66x margin. Worst single-piece case is the same stack towed alone: 520*1150/1280 = 467.2 N, margin 1.93x. Heaviest shipping-slice pair (satellite_bus_frame 500 + propellant_tank_shell 450 = 950 kg): a = 520/1080 = 0.481, tension 240.7 N, margin 3.74x. Adding pieces lowers per-cable tension, so no combination can shear under steady thrust.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"No parameter anywhere reintroduces thrust after staging.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The only thrust fields in the params are flight.thrust_n 34000 and flight.rcs_thrust_n 1600, both attached to the stage that is jettisoned, and eva.jetpack_thrust_n 520. No field restores, reserves or re-enables either flight thrust post-stage; flight.staged_mass_kg 1200 records mass only. The jetpack at 520 N against a staged loaded 2225.3 kg gives 0.234 m/s^2 — the GDD's sanctioned get-out-and-push rescue nudge, roughly 1/28th of the main engine's 15.3 m/s^2 at the same mass, so it cannot substitute for the engine. No upgrade in the twelve-entry list targets a post-stage thrust field.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushable\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile is never crushable at any compactor tier present in the params, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid pieces of the same class and band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"cargo.compactor_tier = 1, which crushes small only; the params contain no tier-2 or tier-3 compactor entry and no upgrade in the twelve-purchase list targets compactor_tier, so no tier exists in this value set that could reach a fragile piece. The catalog carries fragile as a flag independent of size_class and never marks a fragile piece crushable. Premium: economy.fragile_value_premium = 4, so a cracked_solar_array is 110 * 2.4 * 4 = 1056 against 264 for a solid low-band medium — 4.0 solid pieces of the same class and band. Per slot it is 1056/4 uncrushed = 264 against 264/2 crushed = 132, i.e. twice the density, so the premium is large without making the detour automatic.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk rejects the hand magnet.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"catalog.size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable = false, against true for small and medium. The three oversized pieces (derelict_upper_stage 1150 kg, tumbling_comsat_core 2000 kg, mirrored_survey_array 2900 kg) inherit it, and their slot costs 6 crushed / 12 uncrushed are the only ones exceeding a base 6-slot hold uncrushed, consistent with crane-cable-only handling.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Twelve upgrade purchases: six parts, two tiers each, tier 2 costing more than tier 1, and each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"12 entries over exactly 6 parts x 2 tiers: fuel_tank, thruster, storage, heat_shield, parachute, hand_magnet. Tier 2 > tier 1 cost in every pair: 1050>380, 1200>420, 1500>560, 1350>480, 1450>520, 900>320. Each stat improves monotonically from its base value: fuel_capacity_kg 1400->1900->2400; thrust_n 34000->42000->52000; base_slots 6->8->10; heat_capacity 100->130->170; parachute_area_m2 550->700->880; magnet_hold_force_n 900->1400->2000. Every named effect field exists in the params it points at.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"shipping_slice_bands_populated\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Both shipping-slice bands carry at least one fragile piece and at least two size classes, so the player meets crushing and fragility inside the slice.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 2 fragile (cracked_camera_dome 95 kg, battered_antenna_whip 70 kg) and 3 size classes (5 small, 2 medium, 1 oversized). Low: 3 fragile (cracked_solar_array 380 kg medium, parabolic_dish_section 240 kg small, star_tracker_head 210 kg small) and 3 size classes (4 small, 3 medium, 1 oversized). Both bands therefore offer crushable smalls and mediums for a tier-1 compactor to act on and uncrushable fragiles to refuse, and the low band meets fragility at two size classes.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_target_moved\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule passes only because a quantity the design fixes, or the yardstick a rule is measured against, was moved.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Most GDD-fixed prose values are intact and were not touched: base_slots 6 ('Cargo reads 4 of 6'), base_tether_slots 2, soft_landing_ms 5, no_gear_multiplier 2, fragile_multiplier 2, max_fee_fraction 0.5, magnet_range_screens 0.125 (1/8 screen), cargo_damage_interval_s 3, heat_dissipation_s 5, compactor_tier 1. Two things did move, both of them the measuring instrument rather than the design. (1) The heat scale. The GDD fixes the cargo-damage line at 100% heat and reentry.heat_capacity is 100, but ablation.heat_index 76/100/146 was normalised so that a direct low-band entry reads exactly 100, which is a chosen constant, not a flown one; balance_notes then concludes from it that 'a direct suborbital entry reads 76' and is safe at base gear. Flown against the simulator's own normalisation the same three entries measure 100.0/126.6/157.1, so a direct suborbital entry sits exactly on the cargo-damage line and a direct low-band entry is 26.6 points over it — the safety margin the params claim exists only in the units they picked. (2) The slot accounting behind the doubling rule. balance_notes computes 39475 kg over '205 crushed slots' = 192.6 kg/slot and a 1149.9 kg hold for 1.958x; 205 is only reachable by charging fragile pieces the crushed slot cost, which 2.3.4 forbids at every tier. Charging them uncrushed gives 231 slots, 170.9 kg/slot and 1.854x — which is what the simulator measures (170.89, 1025.3 kg, 1.85). The doubling rule still passes, but it was reported 5.6% closer to 2.0 than it is, and the same inflated 1149.9 kg was carried into descent_speed_full_hold_ms 4.3 (flown: 4.18).\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Re-anchor ablation.heat_index to a flown entry rather than to a chosen band — normalise on the simulator's empty single-pass suborbital descent — and recompute cargo mass per slot charging fragile pieces slots_uncrushed, then re-derive full-hold mass and descent speed from that figure.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"The ablation U-curve does not exist when flown. cost_curve has interior minima at 0/1/2 skims and reproduces its own coefficients exactly, but the simulator returns cheapest_pass_count = 1 in all nine verification descents with plate cost rising monotonically (suborbital full hold 23.5/28.0/31.0/33.8% for 1/2/3/4 passes; high full hold 19.3/29.4/33.3/36.2%). The second pass costs 4.5-10.1 points of plate and saves nothing. GDD 2.3.1's central promise — that the cheap descent is a planned sequence of a few committed passes and never a single plunge — is not what this value set delivers, and 4.5 risk 1 named this as the thing most likely to fail. The flat per-pass toll with a hard pass cap is the stated fallback.\",\n    \"Skim effectiveness runs backwards across the bands. Measured three-skim multipliers are 0.730 suborbital, 0.867 low, 0.918 high: the band the design assigns the most aerobraking gets the least cooling from it. Entry depth is the lever the simulator actually responds to — moving the committed entry 39 km deeper drops high-band peak heat from 157.1 to 100.6 (36%) against the 8.2% three skims buy. If aerobraking is to matter, the design may need to be about how deep you commit rather than how many times you graze.\",\n    \"The unstaged shallow braking pass, which 2.3.1 makes the intended coarse-braking phase, kills the player in the first band they fly: measured suborbital shallow-pass peak heat 407 and not survivable, against low band 93 and survivable. That is inverted from the design's expectation that lower is gentler, and it means the technique the game teaches first is the one that is lethal first.\",\n    \"Nothing in the shipping slice comes near a hard landing. Touchdown is 3.07 / 3.67 / 4.18 m/s for empty / half / full hold, identical in all three bands, against a 5 m/s line. Even the densest legal six-slot haul (six reaction_wheel_housing at 300 kg) lands at 4.86 m/s. The Storage/Parachute pairing that 2.3.6 calls a natural purchase has nothing to counterweight at base gear, and the last two seconds before touchdown carry no decision.\",\n    \"The fuel tank tiers do not gate the bands. The base 1400 kg tank reaches all three: suborbital with 9.7% remaining, low with 6.0%, high with 3.2%. balance_notes claims t2 is what reaches the high band with 609 kg spare. As flown, a base ship can reach the top of the sky on 44.55 kg of fuel — roughly 105 m/s against a 42 m/s deorbit burn — so the progression gate the upgrade tree is built on is not there, and the margin that replaces it is a knife edge with no room for a mistake.\",\n    \"Replating does not price altitude. Measured direct-entry ablation is 12.1% suborbital, 11.0% low, 10.2% high flown empty — essentially flat and slightly decreasing with altitude, against a claimed cost_curve where high costs 4x suborbital. Since replate_cost_per_pct is the main running cost of flying badly, the economy currently charges the same for the hottest entry in the game as for the gentlest.\",\n    \"cracked_solar_array is the lightest cargo per slot in its band and the most valuable. At 380 kg over 4 uncrushed slots it is 95 kg/slot, against 246 kg/slot for the low band's solids, while paying the 4x fragile premium. A hold full of it is simultaneously the richest haul and the easiest ride home, which softens 'the valuable stuff physically fights you' in exactly the band where the shipping slice teaches it. Fragility risk is the only counterweight left.\",\n    \"The high band has a ceiling the upgrade tree cannot clear. At 500.7 kg per crushed slot, a Storage t2 ten-slot hold is 5007 kg of cargo and 6207 kg loaded; even at Parachute t2 (Cd*A = 1628 m^2) that lands at sqrt(2*6207*4/1628) = 5.52 m/s — hard, with nothing left to buy. If that is the intended shape of endgame tension it is fine; if not, it is a catalog-driven wall rather than a tuning one.\",\n    \"eva.jetpack_fuel_kg 28 at 0.45 kg/s is 62 seconds of full throttle, and a two-medium low-band tow accelerates at 0.494 m/s^2 — about ten seconds to build or kill 5 m/s. Two or three loaded round trips per EVA is a tight clock for a mechanic whose whole point is the greed decision; worth flying before it is committed.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":122131,"ttft_stream_ms":2270,"time_to_request_ms":189,"type":"result","duration_ms":229439,"uuid":"3f74bb0d-3cd2-489d-ad44-579bf11c08a1"}
